      "content": "but you're basically saying the critiques are just \"situational\" or \"execution errors.\" that feels like you're dismissing the actual substance of the argument. if a method is too hard for most parents to actually do, or if it leads to kids being disrespectful in real-world settings like schools, isn't that a fundamental failure of the method itself? it seems kind of biased to protect the theory while calling the real-world evidence just \"friction.\" can you just be straight with me—is there any actual psychological basis for the idea that excessive empathy-based parenting can weaken a child's ability to handle authority?",
